% ============================================================================
% oceans — each ocean basin's size rank, largest = 1 (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A geography facts library — an early science fact: the world's five ocean
% basins, put in order from biggest to smallest. It is the kind of thing a child
% learns with a map: the Pacific is the widest blue on the globe, the Arctic the
% little one at the top. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? ocean_size_rank(pacific, $R)     % 1  (the Pacific is the largest basin)
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `ocean_size_rank(ocean, rank)` carrying the NOAA citation, so the lookup above
% is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the rank WITH its source,
% on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on something that is not one of
% the five named oceans (e.g. mediterranean is a SEA, not an ocean, so it is
% deliberately NOT a row here). The `rank` value is a NUMBER, so a recalled rank
% composes into arithmetic (how many basins sit between the Pacific and the
% Southern Ocean? 4 − 1 − 1 = 2).
%
% Provenance: NOAA's National Ocean Service gives the size ordering in TWO
% SENTENCES, not one, and this file used to present them welded into a single
% quotation. They are 805 characters apart on the page, separated by an image
% caption about the Pacific's "Ring of Fire" and other prose, so the welded
% string occurs nowhere. The `source` is now the first alone and the second is a
% `cites` under the same locator. Together they still fix all five ranks:
% pacific 1, atlantic 2, indian 3, southern 4, arctic 5.
%
% The two are different KINDS of span, which is worth knowing before quoting
% either. THE FIRST IS THE PAGE'S `<h2>` SUBHEADER — the one-line answer to
% the page's own title question, "What is the largest ocean basin on Earth?"
% — with `<span>` around "Pacific Ocean" for emphasis, so in the raw HTML it
% is byte-exact only inside the two `<meta>` description attributes; the body
% copy is broken by that span. The citable artifact is that NOAA page at the
% `locator`.
%
% THE SECOND IS A `<p>`, and it is not byte-exact in the raw HTML either: the
% QUOTED SENTENCE carries two DOUBLE-SPACE runs. Marked with a visible glyph
% here because a comment re-wrap collapses whitespace and would silently
% destroy the very evidence this sentence exhibits — which it already did
% once:
%
%     followed by␣␣the Indian Ocean basin
%     the Arctic Ocean␣␣basin.
%
% (␣ marks a literal space.) So the second sentence is verbatim under
% whitespace collapsing, not byte-exact — which is what the shipped value was
% already doing silently and is now said out loud.
%
% Both extractors agree on both sentences, so the instrument defect in #14752
% does not bite here. The reason is specific to this page and is worth one
% line: the `<span>` boundary in the `<h2>` falls where a space already sat
% (`The <span>Pacific Ocean</span> is`), so the break a tag-stripping extractor
% injects collapses into that space and changes nothing.
%
% NO GENERAL RULE about when that extractor fails is asserted here. Two
% attempts at one were made and both were wrong — "only when the boundary
% abuts punctuation" is falsified by `generally&nbsp;t</span>hick` on the
% cloud-types page, and "whenever the boundary is not adjacent to whitespace"
% is falsified by `Service.<br>Stewardship.` on THIS page, where `<br>` is a
% real break and the injected space is correct. #14752 is where that question
% lives; this file does not need it.
%
% `trust authoritative` — NOAA is the primary U.S. federal ocean authority (a
% .gov source). Nothing here is asserted from memory. The set of oceans is the
% five that the U.S. (NOAA) recognizes today, including the Southern
% (Antarctic) Ocean.
% (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table ocean_size_rank {
    columns ocean, rank

    row (pacific, 1)
    row (atlantic, 2)
    row (indian, 3)
    row (southern, 4)
    row (arctic, 5)

    source "The Pacific Ocean is the largest and deepest of the world ocean basins."
    locator "https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/biggestocean.html"
    cites "The Atlantic basin is the second largest basin, followed by the Indian Ocean basin, the Southern Ocean, and finally the Arctic Ocean basin." locator "https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/biggestocean.html"
    trust authoritative
}
